{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-refactor` skill MUST be the home of moving code, tests, and requirements to another file, chapter, or outline node, and of renaming requirements.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one obligation: the `habeas-refactor` skill MUST be the home of moving code, tests and requirements to another file, chapter or outline node, and of renaming requirements. \"Home\" comes from the parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure`: each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill.\n\nThat splits into two testable parts: the refactor skill states each topic, and no other skill restates it. The test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_refactor_skill_is_home_of_moves_and_requirement_renames]` covers both.\n\n**The skill states each topic.** Assertions run through `assert_text`, which fails if any phrase is missing.\n- Moving to another chapter: the `## Workflow` section of `habeas-refactor` must contain \"move the `{req}` directive to its new chapter\".\n- Moving a file or directory: the section must contain \"a file or directory: `--map src/config.rs=src/settings.rs`\".\n- Moving to another outline node: the section must contain \"a node: `--map\".\n- Moving a requirement definition: the section must contain \"a definition: `--map requirements.cli.check=requirements.core.check`\".\n- Renaming a requirement: the skill must contain \"Renaming a requirement's ID is a definition mapping\".\n- The `section` helper extracts the `## Workflow` body, so the workflow phrases are checked in the right place. I read `assets/skills/habeas-refactor/SKILL.md`, and it contains all of these phrases.\n\n**Exactly one home.** `assert_home(\"habeas-refactor\")` does two things.\n- It asserts that `habeas-refactor` contains each marker from `home_markers`: \"habeas refactor --dry-run\", the node-map example `--map 'src/config.rs#fn[load]=src/config/load.rs#fn[load]'`, and the \"Would leave ... unchanged\" report description.\n- It loops over the other four skills in `SKILLS`, using `bundled_skill` (the `SKILL.md` plus references), and asserts that none of them contains those markers. A restatement of the refactor procedure elsewhere would therefore fail the test.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. Phrase-based checking is a reasonable way to test a document-structure requirement, and the assertions cover the move targets the text names (file, chapter, node) and renames. The test carries a `{test}` reference to the subject. The requirement has no refining requirement and no FIXME acknowledgments.",
  "references": "a54d07ae817e846f35af61d3ee5e062c81c43bc70e6a4a504a69071debfe727e",
